            "A prime example of an endogenous variable is the price when predicting a product's demand. While it's evident that price can affect demand, demand reciprocally can influence the price. This two-way relationship introduces the simultaneity problem in econometric studies. Overlooking this can result in biased estimates, jeopardizing the accuracy of the analysis. In order to avoid the endogeneity issue, one can use instrumental variables or panel data models (Generalized Method of Moments, Fixed and Random effects, BLP) among others.\n\nAn exogenous variable in this example will be a government-initiated major road infrastructure project, which would independently drive up house prices in a nearby village to a city.",
            "\n            Ops, Strategy & Analytics | Ethical AI & Sustainability Advocate | TEDx Speaker\n          ",
