            "A recent example would be when mapping out the impact of decommissioning the Japanese nuclear power plant on Japan\u2019s sustainable development - the endogenous variables are essentially decommissioned NPP as Beta1, the width of sea land as Beta2 and then any beta (4,5,6,7) depending on what are easier proxies to calculate the impact of NPP on sustainable growth (GDP). All these are endogenous variables.\n\nAnything not considered in this framework is exogenous. \n\nInterestingly - the lower the number of exogenous factors in your equation, the more accurate your p-value may come out to be, as residual values decreases substantially.",
            "\nThe endogeneity problem arises when an endogenous variable is correlated with the error term in a regression model. This violates the assumption of independence between the explanatory variables and the error term, which is essential for obtaining unbiased and consistent estimates of the coefficients. The endogeneity problem can be caused by several factors, such as omitted variables, measurement errors, reverse causality, or simultaneity.\n",
            "\nOne way to test for endogeneity is to use the Hausman test, which compares the estimates obtained from two different models: one that treats the suspected variable as endogenous, and one that treats it as exogenous. The test is based on the idea that if the variable is truly exogenous, then the estimates should not differ significantly between the two models. However, if the variable is endogenous, then the estimates should differ, and the model that treats it as endogenous should be preferred.\n",
